        description: "I independently built a full-stack search engine for exploring NASA’s Image and Video Library, designed to make a large historical media archive easier to search and browse. The application crawls [NASA’s public media API](https://images.nasa.gov) to collect metadata and media links for more than 100,000 images and videos spanning decades of NASA history, then builds a searchable local corpus rather than querying the external API for every user search.\nI developed the data acquisition and information retrieval pipeline in Python. The crawler collects and organizes NASA media metadata, while the indexing pipeline uses spaCy for tokenization, stopword removal, lemmatization, and field weighting before constructing an inverted index. Search queries are processed against this index to efficiently identify candidate documents, which are then ranked using cosine similarity and Okapi BM25. This approach allows searches such as “Apollo 11” or specific mission and spacecraft names to return contextually relevant results without scanning the entire corpus for every query.\nThe backend is implemented with FastAPI and exposes the search functionality and media metadata through HTTP endpoints, while the frontend uses Jinja2 templates and Bootstrap to provide an interactive search experience. I built features including infinite scrolling, date-range and media-type filtering, lazy-loaded result thumbnails, and a full-resolution media viewer with detailed metadata and download links. The application also integrates [NASA’s Astronomy Picture of the Day API](https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/astropix.html) to provide a dynamic home-page feature.\nA major engineering challenge was balancing search performance with the size of the precomputed index and corpus. I stored the full corpus and serialized inverted index locally so the application could load and search the processed data without repeatedly rebuilding the index or making unnecessary API requests. The project gave me experience designing an end-to-end information retrieval pipeline, working with natural language processing, processing large external datasets, building search APIs, and connecting backend search infrastructure to a user-facing web application. The application currently runs locally, with deployment deferred after memory limitations made the large index files impractical for the hosting environment I initially tested.",
