        name: "Traffic Signal Control Using Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning",
        type: "Machine Learning Model",
        brief: "Research-oriented reinforcement learning project focused on using multi-agent reinforcement learning to dynamically control traffic signals across interconnected urban intersections.",
        description: "Research-oriented reinforcement learning project focused on using multi-agent reinforcement learning to dynamically control traffic signals across interconnected urban intersections. Working with a three-person team, I helped design and implement a Multi-Agent Proximal Policy Optimization (MAPPO) system in which each intersection operates as an independent agent, learning traffic signal policies that account for local traffic conditions while coordinating through centralized training.\nI led the initial technical design of the project, researching existing reinforcement learning approaches to traffic signal control and evaluating simulation frameworks before selecting MAPPO and CityFlow for our implementation. Rather than relying on an existing traffic dataset, we generated customized traffic scenarios in [CityFlow](https://cityflow.readthedocs.io/en/latest/#), allowing us to control road-network layouts, traffic flows, vehicle characteristics, and intersection configurations across grids of varying sizes. This gave us a consistent simulation environment for training and evaluating the agents.\nA major part of my implementation was developing the custom reinforcement learning environment that wrapped the CityFlow API and Ray RLlib’s MultiAgentEnv interface. I designed the initial observation space to provide each intersection with local traffic information such as vehicle counts, waiting vehicles, and throughput, and implemented a dynamically normalized reward function to make the environment more adaptable to different grid sizes. The reward formulation combines queue length, waiting time, and throughput to encourage more efficient traffic flow. I also implemented the MAPPO training pipeline in [Ray RLlib](https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/rllib/index.html) after determining that our initial Stable-Baselines3 approach did not adequately support the multi-agent architecture.\nThe training system uses centralized training and decentralized execution: during training, agents can learn from the combined state of the traffic network, while each intersection uses only its local observations when selecting actions. I configured PPO training parameters, trained the agents across hundreds of episodes, logged training and environment metrics, and collaborated on hyperparameter and reward-function tuning using [Ray Tune](https://docs.ray.io/en/latest/tune/index.html). After training, I implemented an evaluation pipeline that runs the learned policy in a new CityFlow simulation and records traffic performance for analysis.\nWe evaluated the learned policy against a fixed-signal baseline using metrics including average waiting time, queue length, throughput, and cumulative reward. Training showed a steady improvement in average reward over the fixed-action baseline, demonstrating that the agents learned policies that improved traffic-flow efficiency. The project also highlighted the challenges of multi-agent reinforcement learning: limited compute resources constrained the amount of hyperparameter tuning and training we could perform, and further training would be required to fully stabilize the model and scale it to substantially larger traffic networks.",
